// CARD a2-01 · SEE: an old globe turning slowly; from one point gold lines shoot out one after another to new places while a counter runs to 42; COUNTRIES BY 1966 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@map_burst;map:stock_world_globe_04;x:.5;y:.5;n:42;go:sold;lab:COUNTRIES BY 1966;in:right"
{excited} The system took off. By nineteen sixty-six, LEGO was sold in forty-two **countries**.

// CARD a2-02 · SEE: the real LEGOLAND Billund of the 1970s: a brick-built train full of passengers on its track, the camera travelling with it · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_legoland_1977_09;ua:0;move:right;z:.4;out:right"
In nineteen sixty-eight, so many visitors turned up at the factory that LEGO built them a park, **LEGOLAND**.

// CARD a2-03 · SEE: the dark frame fills with gold points in waves from one spot, faster and faster, while the count runs to 625,000 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@dots;n:6250;go:drew;lab:625,000 VISITORS;sub:the first season, 1968 · about twice the estimate;in:right"
The first season drew six hundred and twenty-five thousand people, about twice the **estimate**.

// CARD a2-04 · SEE: the camera pushes hard into a real minifigure's smiling yellow face until it fills the frame · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_minifig_head_macro;ua:0;move:in;z:.22;out:right"
Then in nineteen seventy-eight came the **minifigure**.

// CARD a2-05 · SEE: the real Miniland of 1977, a brick town of red roofs, the camera travelling along its empty streets: not one figure in sight · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_legoland_1977;ua:0;move:right;z:.45;in:right"
For forty-six years, LEGO's worlds had had no people in **them**. (J)

// CARD a2-06 · SEE: Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen at a lectern in 1996, the camera travelling to his face; on "Kjeld" a name card slides in at the top · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@name_strap;pic:lego_kjeld_1996_award;go:Kjeld;name:KJELD KIRK KRISTIANSEN;role:the founder's grandson · CEO 1979–2004;fx:.6;fy:.35;out:right"
In nineteen seventy-nine, the founder's grandson, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen, took **charge**.

// CARD a2-07 · SEE: a market stall under a striped canopy hung with hundreds of toys, the camera sliding along it · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_toy_market;move:right;z:.25;in:right"
And by the nineties, LEGO was saying yes to **everything**.

// CARD a2-08 · SEE: hard cuts on the words: a roller coaster on "parks", a clothes rack on "Clothes", a watch face on "Watches", an arcade on "games" · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@word_cuts;items:lego_clip_roller_coaster@parks@.5@.4~lego_clip_clothing_store@Clothes@.5@.45@1.4~lego_clip_watch_face@Watches@.5@.5@1.5~lego_clip_arcade_family@games@.5@.45@1.4;out:right"
More parks. Clothes. Watches. Video **games**. (J)

// CARD a2-09 · SEE: the real LEGO online shop of March 2004 ("The World's Biggest LEGO Shop!") in a browser window, scrolling down through its themes; on "worlds" the headline is highlighted · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@web_scroll;doc:lego_doc_wayback_shop_2004_03_26;crop:0,.41;from:0;to:.22;mark:.11,.155;go:worlds;url:shop.lego.com · archived 26 March 2004;in:right"
New themes, new worlds, new pieces every **year**.

// CARD a2-10 · SEE: a real heap of bricks dim; on "Designers" bricks rain in and pile up while a counter runs from 6,000 to 12,000+ and locks · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@brick_rain;bg:lego_brick_color_pile;go:Designers;n:140;rate:34;from:6000;to:12000;cnt:six;span:2.4;suf:+;lab:DIFFERENT PARTS;out:right"
Designers went from about six thousand different parts to more than twelve **thousand**.

// CARD a2-11 · SEE: a real box of sorted Technic parts behind; a block of 100 tiles in front; on "ninety" 90 of them light up gold, one after another · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@occupancy_grid;plate:lego_technic_bits;pct:90;go:ninety;lab:OF COMPONENTS · IN ONE DESIGN ONLY;sub:case literature (Robertson and Breen);in:right"
And about ninety percent of the components were used in just one **design**.

// CARD a2-12 · MOTIF · SEE: a warehouse buried under a mountain of bricks and boxes (AI moment art); the brick drops in and lands on the heap, one more among thousands; A CATALOGUE sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_scene_brick_warehouse_chaos;go:catalogue;x:.42;y:.6;h:.11;lab:A CATALOGUE;lx:.5;z:.3;move:right;out:right"
{wry} A system where nothing gets reused isn't a system. It's a **catalogue**. (J)

// CARD a2-13 · SEE: a moulding press closing in a factory; on "thousands" a paper price tag swings in on a string in front of it: TENS OF THOUSANDS OF $ · ONE MOULD · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@valuation_tag;bg:lego_clip_shoe_mold;vals:$10,000s@thousands;lab:ONE NEW MOULD;sub:per new shape · case literature;x:.66;in:right"
Every new shape needs its own steel **mould**, and a mould can cost tens of thousands of dollars.

// CARD a2-14 · SEE: a trading screen in a dark room, its chart falling in red steps · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@reel;clip:stock_screen_ticker_18;move:in;z:.2;out:right"
In nineteen ninety-eight, LEGO posted a **loss**.

// CARD a2-15 · SEE: a giant brick-built Millennium Falcon at an exhibition, the camera pushing along it; 1999 · FIRST OUTSIDE LICENCE sets above · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_starwars_millennium_falcon;ua:0;move:in;z:.35;txt:1999 · FIRST OUTSIDE LICENCE;tat:signed;ty:.16;in:right"
In nineteen ninety-nine it signed its first outside licence: Star **Wars**.

// CARD a2-16 · SEE: two framed cards: a brick-built Star Wars AT-AT rises into the light on "money" (STAR WARS · LICENSED); LEGO's own 2002 home page with its Galidor world sinks and drains on "not" · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@rise_and_sink;up:lego_starwars_atat;down:lego_doc_wayback_home_2002_crop;ulab:STAR WARS · LICENSED;dlab:GALIDOR · LEGO'S OWN · 2002;uat:money;dat:not;out:right"
Star Wars made money. But many of LEGO's own new worlds did **not**.
